; host/guards.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Guards`.
;
; `BeamLisp.Guards` is the single place that names the "struct is a map"
; invariant. On the BEAM a struct IS a map, so a bare `is_map` silently accepts
; every one of beam-lisp's struct kinds; `is_bl_map/1` is the name that keeps a
; plain-map collection clause from swallowing them. `is_ref_type/1` names the
; reference structs (which are NOT collections and must raise), and
; `is_data_tuple/1` separates a positional data tuple from the language's own
; tagged-tuple machinery. `internal_tuple_tags/0` is the list that makes the
; last of those decidable, and it is PUBLIC so a caller can read it rather than
; re-list the tags (`rt.bl`'s `tuple-kind` and `priv/host/multi.bl`'s classifier
; are the pinned mirrors of it).
;
; This file is the `.bl` port of `lib/beam_lisp/guards.ex`. It carries no struct
; and no behaviour — the live module has neither — so it is FUNCTIONS-ONLY: the
; whole export surface is the file's top-level defns.
;
; THE MACROS ARE PORTED, and they are the point of the file. `defguard is_bl_map(x)
; when …` emits a compile-time PAIR rather than a runtime function: the
; implementer `MACRO-is_bl_map/2` — Elixir applies it to `(caller-env,
; argument-ast)` when a `require`d (or `import`ed) caller compiles
; `is_bl_map(x)`, and its RETURN VALUE is the quoted guard the caller then
; compiles — plus the `__info__(:macros)` entry `{is_bl_map, 1}`, without which
; `require`/`import` refuse to expand the call at all. The entry is declared
; through `compile_host_file`'s `:macro_exports` option (the seed entry names
; the three); the implementers are below. Nothing else in the tree can stand in
; for them: a pinned file has no `defguard`/`defmacro`, and the ONLY consumer
; that matters is `lib/beam_lisp/record.ex`, which `import`s `is_bl_map/1` and
; uses it in a GUARD (`def map_construct(mod, m) when is_bl_map(m)`).
;
; THE EXPORT SURFACE IS THE LIVE ONE PLUS THIS FILE'S HELPERS. A pinned module
; exports exactly its file's defns, so the AST-building helpers below are
; exports on this beam — the same ONE deliberate divergence `priv/host/decimal.bl`
; and `priv/host/vector.bl` carry for their own helpers. The PUBLIC ABI
; (`internal_tuple_tags/0` plus the three `MACRO-*`/2 implementers, advertised
; through `__info__(:macros)`) is exact.
;
; WHAT EACH IMPLEMENTER RETURNS, AND HOW FAR IT PARTS FROM ELIXIR'S BYTES.
;
;   * `MACRO-is_bl_map/2` returns Elixir's own expansion of
;     `is_map(x) and not is_struct(x)`:
;     `:erlang.andalso(:erlang.is_map(x), :erlang.not(is_struct_ast(x)))` where
;     `is_struct/1` expands to `is_map(x) andalso is_map_key(:__struct__, x)
;     andalso is_atom(map_get(:__struct__, x))`. The ORDER is the order live
;     emits and it is load-bearing: `andalso` SHORT-CIRCUITS, so `map_get/2` is
;     reached only once `x` is known to be a `:__struct__`-carrying map and a
;     non-map fails the guard instead of raising `badmap`.
;   * `MACRO-is_ref_type/2` returns the OR of the five `is_struct(x, Tag)`
;     expansions, LEFT-nested in declaration order, each of them
;     `((is_map(x) andalso (is_atom(Tag) orelse :fail)) andalso
;     is_map_key(:__struct__, x)) andalso map_get(:__struct__, x) == Tag` — byte
;     for byte what Elixir emits, tags written literally (`Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom`,
;     `Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile`, `Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise`,
;     `Elixir.BeamLisp.Future`, `Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced`).
;   * `MACRO-is_data_tuple/2` returns
;     `is_tuple(x) andalso (tuple_size(x) == 0 orelse not (is_atom(element(1, x))
;     andalso (element(1, x) =:= tag_1 orelse … orelse element(1, x) =:= tag_n)))`
;     — `in`'s GUARD form, a left-nested chain of `=:=`, over
;     `internal_tuple_tags/0` IN ORDER. No list and no `:lists.member/2` ever
;     reaches the AST.
;
; THE EXPANSIONS ARE BYTE-SHAPE FAITHFUL, including the one piece of dead code
; Elixir splices into `is_struct/2` — the per-tag `orelse(is_atom(Literal),
; :fail)` assertion. It asserts a literal the caller cannot vary and folds away
; to nothing, so eliding it would cost nothing at runtime; it is KEPT because
; here the expansion IS the deliverable, and the port's own test then diffs the
; EMITTED CALL SPINE against the live macro's exactly, with no normalisation to
; hide a difference behind. (`priv/host/decimal.bl` elides the same assertion
; for its own `is_struct(x, __MODULE__)` guard, and says so: there the macro is
; a convenience over a predicate whose expansion nobody observes, while here it
; is the public ABI itself.)
;
; ONE DIVERGENCE, and it is not in the AST: in a GUARD the argument AST is
; spliced in once per test, exactly as Elixir does — a guard is pure, so
; re-reading the argument cannot change the answer — while OUTSIDE a guard a
; second read of a side-effecting argument WOULD be observable, so the value is
; bound once to a hygienic variable and the tests read THAT. That is Elixir's
; own two-branch shape, selected by `Macro.Env.in_guard?/1` on the caller
; environment.
;
; THE GUARD IT RETURNS IS DATA. Every node built here is an AST for the CALLER's
; compiler to expand, never a call made here — so no `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` or
; runtime var appears in it, and the same bytes serve a `when` clause, an `if`
; condition and a plain expression. This function runs only when an Elixir
; compiler expands the macro, never on the boot path the rest of this file is
; held to, which is why it may name `:elixir_env` and `Elixir.Macro.Env`.
;
; BOOT SAFETY — THE PRECOMPILE EDGE. `record.ex` `import`s this module and
; `elixirc` must resolve `is_bl_map/1` to a `__info__(:macros)` entry on a beam
; that carries `MACRO-is_bl_map/2`. That compile happens BEFORE the language is
; booted, so nothing here may depend on a namespace or a var the boot has not
; interned yet: every body is written against `:erlang` BIFs, `:lists` and the
; one Elixir-internal normalizer (`:elixir_env`), the same discipline
; `priv/host/decimal.bl` holds to. In particular the tag list is built with
; `make_tuple`/`setelement`/`tuple_to_list`, NOT with `(list …)`/`(tuple …)`,
; which lower to `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` calls the compiler's boot has not reached.
; The result is a beam whose only remote targets outside ITSELF are `:erlang`,
; `:lists`, `:elixir_env` and `Elixir.Macro.Env` — the module's own atom appears
; because the pinned emitter retargets the file's sibling calls at the pinned
; module — and that whole set is asserted from the disassembly, not from this
; prose.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_guards_test.bl

(ns host.guards)

; ── module-exact self reference ───────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- self_mod
  "The atom THIS source was compiled into, read from a fun literal compiled into
   this file — module-exact, so the same source serves the production atom in
   the seed and a probe atom under test without naming either. It is also the
   HYGIENE CONTEXT of the variable the non-guard expansion binds."
  []
  (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))

; ── tuples and lists from `:erlang` primitives only ───────────────────────
;
; beam-lisp's `(tuple …)`/`(list …)` are `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` calls, and this file
; is resolved by `elixirc` before the boot has interned Core (see the header).
; These build the same data out of `make_tuple`/`setelement`/`tuple_to_list`,
; the `priv/host/decimal.bl` idiom.

(defn- tup2 [a b]
  (erlang/setelement 2 (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 2 :undefined) a) b))

(defn- tup3 [a b c]
  (erlang/setelement 3
    (erlang/setelement 2
      (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 3 :undefined) a) b) c))

(defn- tup5 [a b c d e]
  (erlang/setelement 5
    (erlang/setelement 4
      (erlang/setelement 3
        (erlang/setelement 2
          (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 5 :undefined) a) b) c) d) e))

(defn- tup10 [a b c d e f g h i j]
  (erlang/setelement 10
    (erlang/setelement 9
      (erlang/setelement 8
        (erlang/setelement 7
          (erlang/setelement 6
            (erlang/setelement 5
              (erlang/setelement 4
                (erlang/setelement 3
                  (erlang/setelement 2
                    (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 10 :undefined) a) b) c) d) e) f) g) h) i) j))

(defn- singleton [x]
  (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 1 :undefined) x))

(defn- lst1 [a] (erlang/tuple_to_list (singleton a)))
(defn- lst2 [a b] (erlang/tuple_to_list (tup2 a b)))
(defn- lst3 [a b c] (erlang/tuple_to_list (tup3 a b c)))

; ── the tag authority ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn internal_tuple_tags
  "The tuple tags that mark a value as language machinery, not data — the live
   module's `@internal_tuple_tags`, in the same ORDER (the order is observable:
   `is_data_tuple/1`'s guard folds its `=:=` chain over this list, so a
   reordering changes the emitted AST).

   NOT here, deliberately: `:bl_set` / `:bl_set_ack` / `:bl_deref` / `:bl_value`
   — those are MESSAGES between a ref and its holder process (see `refs.bl`),
   never values a user holds. Excluding them would have made
   `(first (tuple :bl_set x))` — an ordinary, spellable data tuple —
   mysteriously opaque.

   Adding a new tagged-tuple representation means adding it here. This list is
   the single place that decides what is data and what is machinery, and it is
   PUBLIC so a caller can read it rather than re-list the tags: `rt.bl`'s
   `tuple-kind` and `priv/host/multi.bl`'s classifier both MIRROR it by hand
   (they are pinned files that cannot `import` a macro, and the mirror is a
   `cond` they each own), so the guard is the authority and they are the
   copies. The PINNED test asserts this list's ORDER too, because
   `is_data_tuple/1` folds its `=:=` chain over it."
  []
  (erlang/tuple_to_list
    (tup10 :"$blfn" :"$remote" :"$macro" :"$protocol" :"$transient"
           :symbol :meta :bl_deftype :bl_reify :bl_vec)))

; ── AST construction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A macro's contract is a TERM, not a call: these build Elixir AST nodes as
; plain tuples (never a `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` `(tuple …)`).

(defn- ast_node
  "One Elixir AST node: `{form, meta, args}`."
  [form meta args]
  (erlang/list_to_tuple (lst3 form meta args)))

(defn- ast_call
  "The Elixir AST for the remote call `mod.fun(args)`:
   `{{:., [], [mod, fun]}, [], args}`. `meta` is stamped on BOTH nodes — which
   is how `generated: true` reaches the compiler in the non-guard branch (see
   `bind_once_ast`)."
  [meta mod fun args]
  (ast_node (ast_node :"." meta (lst2 mod fun)) meta args))

(defn- g_is_map [meta x] (ast_call meta :erlang :is_map (lst1 x)))
(defn- g_is_map_key [meta k x] (ast_call meta :erlang :is_map_key (lst2 k x)))
(defn- g_map_get [meta k x] (ast_call meta :erlang :map_get (lst2 k x)))
(defn- g_is_atom [meta x] (ast_call meta :erlang :is_atom (lst1 x)))
(defn- g_is_tuple [meta x] (ast_call meta :erlang :is_tuple (lst1 x)))
(defn- g_tuple_size [meta x] (ast_call meta :erlang :tuple_size (lst1 x)))
(defn- g_element [meta n x] (ast_call meta :erlang :element (lst2 n x)))
(defn- g_andalso [meta a b] (ast_call meta :erlang :andalso (lst2 a b)))
(defn- g_orelse [meta a b] (ast_call meta :erlang :orelse (lst2 a b)))
(defn- g_not [meta a] (ast_call meta :erlang :not (lst1 a)))
(defn- g_eq [meta a b] (ast_call meta :erlang :== (lst2 a b)))
(defn- g_strict_eq [meta a b] (ast_call meta :erlang :"=:=" (lst2 a b)))

(defn- is_struct_ast
  "Elixir's `Kernel.is_struct/1`, exactly as a `defguard` expands it:
   `is_map(x) andalso is_map_key(:__struct__, x) andalso
   is_atom(map_get(:__struct__, x))`. A map whose `:__struct__` is a NON-atom is
   a plain map by this test — which is why the tag has to be an atom for a
   struct to count."
  [meta x]
  (g_andalso meta
    (g_andalso meta (g_is_map meta x) (g_is_map_key meta :__struct__ x))
    (g_is_atom meta (g_map_get meta :__struct__ x))))

(defn- tagged_ast
  "Elixir's `Kernel.is_struct(x, tag)`, byte for byte — including the
   compile-time-only `orelse(is_atom(tag), :fail)` assertion Elixir splices in.
   It is dead code (it asserts a literal the caller cannot vary, and folds away
   to nothing), and it is KEPT rather than elided: this port's deliverable IS
   the expansion a `require`ing caller compiles, so the shape it emits is the
   shape live emits, and the test's expansion-class diff is exact rather than
   normalised. `andalso` short-circuits, so `map_get/2` is reached only once `x`
   is known to carry the `:__struct__` key."
  [meta x tag]
  (g_andalso meta
    (g_andalso meta
      (g_andalso meta (g_is_map meta x)
                 (g_orelse meta (g_is_atom meta tag) :fail))
      (g_is_map_key meta :__struct__ x))
    (g_eq meta (g_map_get meta :__struct__ x) tag)))

(defn- is_bl_map_ast
  "The guard recognising a beam-lisp map: `is_map(x) and not is_struct(x)`. The
   inner `is_map` is not redundant — it is the one `is_struct/1` itself opens
   with, and live emits it too."
  [meta x]
  (g_andalso meta (g_is_map meta x) (g_not meta (is_struct_ast meta x))))

(defn- ref_tags
  "The five reference struct tags, in the live module's declaration order —
   `is_struct(x, BeamLisp.Atom) or … or is_struct(x, BeamLisp.Reduced)`. Built
   from a tuple rather than a `(list …)` literal, which would be a
   `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` call this file cannot make (see the header)."
  []
  (erlang/tuple_to_list
    (tup5 :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Atom" :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Volatile"
          :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Promise" :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Future"
          :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Reduced")))

(defn- is_ref_type_ast
  "The guard recognising a reference struct: the OR of the five
   `is_struct(x, Tag)` expansions, LEFT-nested in the live module's order — the
   nesting `or` itself produces, so the emitted call spine is the live one."
  [meta x]
  (lists/foldl
    (fn [tag acc]
      (let [t (tagged_ast meta x tag)]
        (if (erlang/== :undefined acc) t (g_orelse meta acc t))))
    :undefined
    (ref_tags)))

(defn- tags_member_ast
  "`element(1, x) in @internal_tuple_tags`, in the shape `in` takes inside a
   GUARD: a left-nested chain of `=:=` comparisons against the tag literals, in
   list order. No list term and no `:lists.member/2` reaches the AST — the
   chain is unrolled at expansion time, exactly as Elixir unrolls it."
  [meta head]
  (lists/foldl
    (fn [tag acc]
      (let [eq (g_strict_eq meta head tag)]
        (if (erlang/== :undefined acc) eq (g_orelse meta acc eq))))
    :undefined
    (internal_tuple_tags)))

(defn- is_data_tuple_ast
  "The guard recognising a data tuple: a tuple that is not one of the language's
   tagged values. `tuple_size(x) == 0` short-circuits the `element(1, x)` read,
   so an empty tuple is data (it has no tag to classify) and nothing raises."
  [meta x]
  (g_andalso meta
    (g_is_tuple meta x)
    (g_orelse meta
      (g_eq meta (g_tuple_size meta x) 0)
      (g_not meta
        (g_andalso meta
          (g_is_atom meta (g_element meta 1 x))
          (tags_member_ast meta (g_element meta 1 x)))))))

(defn- bound_var_ast
  "The variable a non-guard expansion introduces: `{arg1, [generated: true],
   THIS MODULE}`. The module as the name's CONTEXT is what makes it hygienic —
   it cannot collide with a caller's own `arg1` — and `generated` keeps the
   compiler from warning about a binding the caller never wrote."
  []
  (ast_node :arg1 (lst1 (tup2 :generated true)) (self_mod)))

(defn- bind_once_ast
  "The NON-GUARD expansion: read the argument ONCE, then run the guard tests on
   the binding. See the header's divergence for why this branch exists.

   Every node it builds is stamped `generated: true`. That is Elixir's own mark
   for code the caller did not write, and it is load-bearing rather than
   cosmetic: `:erlang.andalso/2` is an OPERATOR, not an exported function, so a
   remote call to it in a non-guard position is `undefined or private` as far as
   the compiler's function check is concerned and earns a warning — one the
   reference expansion never shows because all of its generated nodes carry the
   same stamp."
  [x which]
  (let [gen (lst1 (tup2 :generated true))
        v (bound_var_ast)
        guard (cond
                (erlang/== which :is_bl_map) (is_bl_map_ast gen v)
                (erlang/== which :is_ref_type) (is_ref_type_ast gen v)
                :else (is_data_tuple_ast gen v))]
    (ast_node :__block__ gen
      (lst2 (ast_node := gen (lst2 v x)) guard))))

(defn- expand
  "The shared implementer body: `(caller-env, argument-ast)` in, the quoted
   guard out. `:elixir_env/to_caller` is Elixir's own normalizer for its two
   caller shapes (the compiler's `{line, compile-state, %Macro.Env{}}`, a tool's
   bare `%Macro.Env{}`) — exactly what `defguard`'s generated code calls — and
   `Elixir.Macro.Env/in_guard?/1` is public API."
  [caller x which]
  (let [env (elixir_env/to_caller caller)]
    (if (Elixir.Macro.Env/in_guard? env)
      (cond
        (erlang/== which :is_bl_map) (is_bl_map_ast (quote ()) x)
        (erlang/== which :is_ref_type) (is_ref_type_ast (quote ()) x)
        :else (is_data_tuple_ast (quote ()) x))
      (bind_once_ast x which))))

; ── the `defguard` macro ABI ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; `defguard is_bl_map/1`'s compile-time artifacts: the implementer
; `MACRO-is_bl_map/2` (below) plus the `__info__(:macros)` entry
; `{is_bl_map, 1}` the seed's `:macro_exports` synthesizes.

(defn MACRO-is_bl_map
  "`defguard is_bl_map/1`'s implementer: `(caller-env, argument-ast)` in, the
   quoted guard out, for the caller's compiler to expand. The predicate is
   `is_map(x) and not is_struct(x)`."
  [caller x]
  (expand caller x :is_bl_map))

(defn MACRO-is_ref_type
  "`defguard is_ref_type/1`'s implementer. The predicate is the OR of
   `is_struct(x, Tag)` over the four reference structs plus `Reduced`."
  [caller x]
  (expand caller x :is_ref_type))

(defn MACRO-is_data_tuple
  "`defguard is_data_tuple/1`'s implementer. The predicate is
   `is_tuple(x) and (tuple_size(x) == 0 or not (is_atom(element(1, x)) and
   element(1, x) in @internal_tuple_tags))`."
  [caller x]
  (expand caller x :is_data_tuple))
